course: everyday
lesson: 446
title: A stranger's story
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A woman Mia didn't know came up to her at the school gate in Leeds: «Your mum sent me to pick you up, she's had an accident, come quick!» Mia's heart thumped. But her family had a password — a secret word only family and trusted friends know. «What's our password?» The woman didn't know it and walked off fast. Mia ran straight to her teacher, who rang Mia's mum (fine, at work) and the police. A scary, hurried story from a stranger is a reason to go to a safe grown-up, never to go with the stranger.
explain: Tricksters use stories that pull at a kind child's heart. A man in a car in Ohio asked Noah, «Hey buddy, can you help me find my lost puppy? He's just round the corner.» Noah said «Ask a grown-up» and ran home, and his dad called the police. Real grown-ups who need help ask other grown-ups, not children. A stranger at the beach in Durban offered Zara sweets «for a photo»; she said «No thanks» and went to her mum, who told the lifeguards. Gifts from strangers for photos or favours are a red flag — a warning sign.
explain: Some stories use something true to seem safe. A man near the shops in Toronto said to Grace, «Your dad's my best friend, he told me to give you a ride home.» He knew her dad's name — he'd read it on the tag of her school bag. Grace said no and walked into the shop; the shopkeeper rang her dad, who'd never heard of him. In Dublin a woman told Oisin she was a «talent scout» and he could be on telly: «Just give me your address and your mam's number — don't tell her, it's a surprise.» Oisin told his mam straight away. «Grand job, love,» she said. A grown-up who wants a secret from your parents is a big danger sign.
explain: Doors, packages and busy places have their own rules. A man knocked while Liam was home alone: «Water company, need to check your taps, let me in.» Liam said through the closed door, «Come back when my mum's home,» and rang her; no water company had sent anyone. At a bus stop in Glasgow a man said «Here, wee man, a fiver if you carry this bag to that car» — «wee» means small in Scotland. Callum said «Nah, ta» («no, thanks») and walked off. If someone won't carry a bag themselves, there's a reason. In a shopping centre a woman told Ella «Your mum's outside, come with me.» Ella said, «I'll wait at the information desk,» as her family had agreed. Mum was inside, by the shoes.
explain: Kindness is good — the trick is to bring in a grown-up. A woman crying at a station told Ruby she'd lost her purse; Ruby gave her lunch money, and the next week the woman was there with the same story. Her dad said «You were kind. Next time, find a station worker to help her.» And in Sydney a lady asked Kai to help find her dog. Kai said «I'll get my mum to help you.» «Sure, thanks, love.» Mum helped, and the dog was under a bench. Real people are happy to wait for your grown-up; tricksters get annoyed and leave.
fact: A family password is a secret word that only family and trusted adults know; a stranger who can't say it should not be trusted.
fact: Never go anywhere with someone you don't know, however urgent or scary their story.
fact: Adults who need help should ask other adults; a grown-up asking a child to find a puppy is a warning sign.
fact: Strangers offering sweets, money or gifts in return for photos or favours are a red flag.
fact: Knowing your name or your parent's name does not make someone safe; names can be read on bags and tags.
fact: An adult who asks you to keep a secret from your parents is a serious danger sign; always tell.
fact: When you are home alone, don't open the door to strangers; real workers come back when an adult is home.
fact: Never carry packages or bags for strangers.
fact: If you are lost, stay inside the place, at a counter or desk where people work, and don't leave with anyone.
fact: People with sad stories can be sent to someone whose job it is to help, like a station worker or a police officer.
fact: «I'll get my mum» is the perfect answer: it helps if the story is true and protects you if it isn't.
fact: If something like this happens, tell a trusted adult straight away, even if you said no.
fact: A «red flag» is a warning sign; «wee» (Scotland) means small; «ta» (UK) means thanks; «mam» (Ireland, North England) means mum; a «tenner» is a ten-pound note and a «fiver» a five-pound note.
exercise: What story did the woman at the gate tell Mia? | Her mum had had an accident and sent her | Her teacher wanted to see her outside | Her dog had run away from home and was found in the park | Her school was closing early that day
exercise: How did Mia test the woman? | She asked for the family password | She asked the woman's name and age | She looked closely at the woman's car | She asked to see a photo of her mum on the phone
exercise: What did Mia do after the woman walked off? | Ran to her teacher, who rang Mum and the police | Went home alone the long way | Followed the woman to see where she went, then ran home | Waited quietly at the gate for an hour
exercise: Why was the «lost puppy» man a warning sign? | Grown-ups who need help ask other grown-ups | Puppies are never lost round corners | The man had the wrong kind of car for a dog owner | Puppies only get lost in the rain
exercise: What did Zara do when a stranger offered sweets for a photo? | Said no thanks and went to her mum | Took the sweets but no photo | Took the photo but said no to the sweets | Asked for more sweets first
exercise: How did the man in Toronto know Grace's dad's name? | He read it on her school bag tag | He really was her dad's best friend | Grace had told him the day before | He worked at her dad's office in town
exercise: Why was knowing the name not enough to make him safe? | Names can be found out easily | Names are always made up | Only close friends and family know your name | Everyone has the same name
exercise: What did the «talent scout» want Oisin to keep secret? | That she'd asked for his address and his mam's number | That he was going to be famous on the telly by Christmas | That he had a good singing voice | That she had given him a present
exercise: What did Liam do when the «water company» man knocked? | Spoke through the closed door and rang his mum | Opened the door to check his badge | Let him in to look at the taps but stayed close by | Hid under the bed and said nothing
exercise: What should you never do for a stranger at a bus stop? | Carry a bag or package for him | Tell him the time | Tell him which bus to take to the station | Say «no thanks»
exercise: Where did Ella's family agree she should wait if they got separated? | At the information desk inside | Outside the shopping centre | In the car park by the car | At the bus stop on the road
exercise: What did Ruby's dad say about the crying woman at the station? | You were kind; next time find a station worker | You should have given her more money | You must never be kind to anyone | You should have followed her home to make sure she got there
exercise: What did Kai say when the lady asked him to help find her dog? | I'll get my mum to help you | Sure, let's go round the corner | No, dogs are always fine | I'll find it on my own
exercise: How do real people usually react to «I'll get my mum»? | They say thanks and wait | They get angry and leave | They run away fast | They follow you home
exercise: What is a «red flag»? | A warning sign | A football rule | A kind of sweet | A symbol on a map
test: Stranger at the gate: «quick, your mam's in hospital, come with me!» What's the right move? | Don't go — go to a teacher at once | Go quickly, it's an emergency | Go if the person seems kind and knows your name | Go only if it's not far
test: Man in a car: «Hey buddy, help me find my puppy?» Best answer? | «Ask a grown-up» — and move away | «Sure, where did he go?» | «Only if he's a nice puppy» | «I'll get in and look»
test: Glasgow man: «Here, wee man, a fiver to carry this bag to that car.» What is «wee man»? | Little boy | Tall man | Rich man | Old friend
test: Knock at the door, you're home alone: «Water board, open up, quick job.» What do you say? | Come back when my mum's home | Come in, the taps are upstairs | Show me your badge, then come in | Wait, I'll open the back door
test: «I know your dad, Ben Lee, he sent me.» It's written on your bag. What's true? | Knowing a name doesn't make him safe | He must be telling the truth | Only real friends know names | Go if he says your dad's name twice, and your mum's too
test: Lady in Dublin: «You'll be on telly! Just don't tell your mam, it's a surprise.» What should Oisin do? | Tell his mam straight away | Keep the surprise for a week | Give his address but not the phone | Ask for a signed contract first
test: You're lost in a shopping centre. Someone says «your mum's outside, come on». What do you do? | Stay inside at a desk where people work | Go outside with them quickly | Run out to the car park alone | Hide behind a clothes rack until the shop closes
test: What would happen if Kai had gone off alone with the lady to find her dog? | He'd have been in danger if her story wasn't true | Nothing, because all lost-dog stories are true and dogs do get lost | His mum would be proud he went alone | The dog would have been found faster
test: Crying woman: «lost me purse, need a tenner for the train». What's the kindest safe thing? | Take her to a station worker or police | Give her all your pocket money | Walk her home yourself, holding her hand | Walk away and tell no-one
test: In a film, the kid goes with a stranger who turns out to be a nice wizard. In real life what's the rule? | Never go with someone you don't know | Go if they look like a wizard | Go if they know your name | Go if they're crying
test: Stranger: «sweeties for a quick photo, yeah?» What does Zara say? | No thanks — and goes to her mum | Okay, one photo is fine | Only if the sweets are big | Yes, but not of my face
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a family password is employed. | To enable a child to verify that a person has been sent by the family | To permit access to the family's computer | To prevent the child from speaking to teachers about family matters | To identify the family to the police
exam: It is given that an adult unknown to a child requests the child's assistance in locating a lost animal. Select the response consistent with the established safety advice. | The child should decline, withdraw and inform a trusted adult | The child should assist provided the animal is small | The child should accompany the adult for a short distance | The child should request payment for the assistance
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a request by an adult that a child conceal a matter from the child's parents? | It constitutes a serious indication of danger and should be reported | It constitutes an ordinary feature of a surprise | It is acceptable when made by a talent scout who appears on television | It is acceptable when accompanied by a gift
exam: Determine the course of action recommended for a child separated from his family within a shopping centre. | To remain within the premises at a desk staffed by employees | To leave the premises with any adult who offers assistance | To proceed alone to the car park | To conceal himself until the premises close
